  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nOur current synoptic pattern shows an upper ridge with respect to a \n500mb high pressure system off of the Texas Gulf Coast. This is what \nhas been responsible for higher temperatures off to the south, along \nwith southerly flow bringing higher moisture to our west. At the \nsurface, a high pressure system centered northeast of the Great \nLakes has allowed for easterly flow to bring a more fall-like \npattern across the area. Our dry, pleasant fall-like conditions will \nstick around until tonight, before a shortwave brings a wetter \nFriday and weekend. Far NW MO will get a small taste this\nevening as a passing shortwave brings some scattered sprinkles \nto showers during the evening.\n\nOff the west coast, we have several synoptic features that will have \na direct effect on our weather Friday, and into this weekend. \nAn area of low pressure is present across parts of the Pacific \nNorthwest, along with another low feature in the Baja California\nregion. These two systems merge will create as shortwave to \ndevelop over the Rocky Mountain region tonight, and will \nprogress into our area tomorrow. This will be our lifting \nmechanism responsible for a cloudy, cooler, and rainier \nconditions Friday through the weekend. Additionally, an ongoing\ntemperature gradient is present across Missouri, with \ntemperatures in the 60s to the north, and 80s to the south. This\nis being caused by several features, such as easterly flow \nbringing cooler air across the north, and the position of the \nridge is expected to open up southerly warm air flow. The \ncombined will bring enough lift for on and off shower activity \nbeginning Thursday evening across far northwestern Missouri, \nand continue to progress eastward to the rest of the CWA by \nmidday Friday. Highest coverage is expected along and north of \nInterstate 70 due to greater moisture content across eastern KS \nand northern. Eastward, more spares coverage of rain and storms \nare expected. Cloud cover and morning rainfall keeps \ntemperatures lower as well as limit instability; therefore, \nreducing chances for seeing widespread thunderstorm activity. \nThat said, there looks to be enough forcing to facilitate a few \nembedded thunderstorms. Main hazards would be localized heavy \ndownpours, isolated minor flooding, and possibly some gusty \nwinds. Rainy and gloomy conditions will persist throughout \nFriday night, Saturday, and into Sunday with rain looking to \nvacate by midday Sunday for most of the area. How quickly the \nprecipitation departs will be dependent on how the ongoing \nblocking pattern builds or breaks down through the day. A \ncurrent low off of the East Coast is expected to develop into a \nnotable Nor'easter, and upper flow associated with it will \ninfluence how the ongoing ridge progresses. If the Nor'easter \nsystem and blocking pattern were to linger around longer, it \npotentially could keep our ridge to the south, keeping shower \nactivity around longer on Sunday, and have a cooler Monday. If \nthe Nor'easter and blocking pattern moves out/breaks down, the \nridge would be able to push shower activity northward out of the\narea, and lead to higher temperatures Monday. \n\n\nBy the end of the weekend into early next week, another upper\nlevel disturbance arrives on shore the Pacific coast as the \nblocking pattern exits the eastern CONUS. Timing will be \ndependent on when exactly our ongoing patterns move out, but \nthe next disturbance is expected to move in some time Monday \nnight through Wednesday. The system would bring shower and storm\nactivity, along with cooler and more fall-like conditions \nthrough the end of the forecast period.",
